from assessment_writer import write_review
def compact(sid,title,rows,appraisal):
    write_review(sid,title,[(a,b.split(' | '),c) for a,b,c in rows],appraisal)

compact('MF-NRG-LU001-2021','NRG-LU001: no survival benefit from adding metformin to chemoradiation',[
('Objectives; Methods','Test metformin added to chemoradiation in stage III NSCLC. | Prespecified primary one-year progression-free survival. | Cancer control and survival.','Not a trial of diabetes management.'),
('Methods; Table 1; Figure 1','Adults with unresectable stage IIIA/IIIB NSCLC without diabetes. | Zubrod performance status 0–1; 79 institutions in US, Canada and Israel. | 170 enrolled, three ineligible excluded; 86 metformin and 81 control analyzed.','The eligible ITT population is 167.'),
('Treatment','Oral metformin tablets. | Escalated over two weeks to 2000 mg/day during chemoradiation and consolidation chemotherapy. | Both groups received 60 Gy radiation, weekly carboplatin/paclitaxel and two consolidation cycles. | Median follow-up among survivors 27.7 months.','This regimen predates routine consolidation immunotherapy.'),
('Randomization','Same chemoradiation without metformin. | Central 1:1 assignment stratified by performance, histology and stage. | Zelen permuted-block scheme. | Open-label; central imaging reviewer masked to assignment. | Concurrent randomized comparison.','Masked imaging review reduces but does not eliminate open-label limitations.'),
('Statistical analysis','Primary PFS; secondary overall survival, recurrence and toxicity. | RECIST 1.1 progression or death; CTCAE 4.03 toxicity. | One-year primary estimate, longer time-to-event follow-up. | Clinical cancer endpoints. | Design targeted 15-point one-year PFS improvement, 50% to 65%; not a universal MCID.','Do not equate response biomarkers with these outcomes.'),
('Table 2; Survival outcomes','One-year PFS 51.3% metformin versus 60.4% control; OS 80.8% versus 80.2%. | Eligible population 86 versus 81; survival percentages are Kaplan-Meier estimates, not simple event fractions. | PFS HR 1.15, CI 0.77–1.73; OS HR 1.03, CI 0.64–1.68. | Estimated PFS difference −9.1 percentage points, calculated from rounded reported rates. | Primary reported P=.24; blinded imaging sensitivity HR 1.09, CI 0.69–1.73.','No survival benefit demonstrated; uncertainty does not establish equivalence.'),
('Protocol adherence','Radiation received by 74/86 versus 75/81. | Time-to-event censoring; not all randomized patients received protocol therapy. | 52 patients, 63.4% of the reported adherence denominator, completed the metformin course; only 39% maintained full dose without modification. | Three eligibility exclusions after randomization. | Eligible ITT for efficacy, treated safety population. | Individual patient.','Treatment completion and outcome availability must remain distinct.'),
('Adverse events; Table 3','CTCAE assessment at follow-up; protocol-related toxicity tabulated. | 79 metformin and 75 control. | At least one grade 3+ event: 52/79 versus 51/75; grade 5 events one versus four, none deemed potentially treatment-related. | Metformin adverse effects were the most common reason for its discontinuation. | During treatment and scheduled follow-up.','Comparable severe-event rates do not mean no gastrointestinal burden.'),
('Methods; limitations','Open-label, phase II sample and incomplete full-dose exposure; masked imaging a strength. | Randomization limits confounding; background care and adherence affect interpretation. | GAP: Full separately linked protocol and supplementary tables not acquired in this packet. | Several secondary comparisons; avoid selecting isolated trends. | Wide efficacy intervals; trial designed for a large improvement. | Abstract age IQR differs slightly from full-cohort table; no harmonization needed for guide conclusions.','No validated subgroup rescue of the null result.'),
('Eligibility; Discussion','Unresectable stage III NSCLC without diabetes. | 2000 mg/day with specified carboplatin/paclitaxel regimen. | Does not support adding metformin to improve survival. | Not all lung cancer stages, molecular therapies or modern immunotherapy combinations were tested.','Do not generalize a regimen-specific null to diabetes treatment.'),
('NCT02186847; current inventory','NRG-LU001 independent trial. | ALMERA is a separate Canadian trial with different chemotherapy and duration. | No duplicate cohort identified between these named trials. | Current guide sources, not exhaustive NSCLC review.','Both should be presented without pooling incompatible primary endpoints.'),
('Byline; disclosures','Heath Skinner, Chen Hu, Theodoros Tsakiridis and colleagues. | NRG Oncology multicenter network. | NCI grants; NRG/NCI involved in design, conduct, analysis and publication. | NR: Donated-product source not established in inspected text. | Several investigators disclosed grants, consulting and other industry relationships.','Disclosures are documented, not used as a substitute for appraisal.'),
('Full report','Published JAMA Oncology 2021, DOI 10.1001/jamaoncol.2021.2318. | Full article including tables acquired. | GAP: Supplement 1 protocol and Supplement 2 not separately recovered. | NCT02186847 identified in report; separate registry not audited here. | No correction/retraction identified in retrieved records. | November 3–4, 2026.','Main-report assessment supports the bounded null conclusion.'),
('G-METFORMIN lung-cancer chapter','Adding metformin to chemoradiation. | No demonstrated PFS or OS benefit. | Authors\' clinical conclusion agrees with observed comparisons; do not convert mechanistic rationale into efficacy.','Retain dose-completion limitations.'),
('Synthesis','Routine anticancer addition is not supported by this trial. | Other modern regimens remain separate questions. | Direct randomized evidence in those regimens would be necessary.','Do not infer cancer benefit from glucose lowering.')
], 'Randomized phase II evidence did not show improved progression-free or overall survival. Open-label treatment, incomplete dosing and broad confidence intervals limit precision, while blinded imaging review supports the robustness of the null comparison.')

compact('MF-ALMERA-2021','ALMERA: worse outcomes and more severe toxicity in a small randomized trial',[
('Objectives; Methods','Test concurrent and maintenance metformin with chemoradiation. | Primary one-year treatment-failure proportion. | Failure includes progression, death or discontinuation of trial treatment/evaluations.','This endpoint is not conventional PFS.'),
('Methods; Results','Adults with unresected stage III NSCLC without diabetes. | Seven Canadian institutions. | 54 randomized: 26 metformin, 28 control; one per arm withdrew before treatment.','Accrual was below the planned approximately 96 participants.'),
('Interventions','Metformin supplied as oral study medication. | Escalated to 2000 mg/day during chemoradiation, then up to 12 months. | Platinum chemotherapy, mainly cisplatin/etoposide, plus 60–63 Gy radiation; later amendments allowed consolidation and durvalumab. | Primary 12 months, survival follow-up to 24 months.','Regimen differs from NRG-LU001.'),
('Study design; patient population','Chemoradiation without metformin. | Central OCOG randomization, stratified by stage and later consolidation chemotherapy. | NR: Sequence-generation detail not fully provided in main report. | Open-label; outcome adjudication committee masked. | Recruitment 2014–2019; stopped for slow accrual.','Stopping for accrual is distinct from an efficacy stopping rule.'),
('Assessments and outcomes','Primary binary failure; conventional PFS/OS secondary. | CT imaging and clinical evaluation; CTCAE 4.03 for grade 3+ events. | Failure by 12 months. | Clinical composite includes treatment feasibility as well as cancer events. | NR: No independently validated patient-important threshold for this composite.','A discontinuation is not necessarily cancer progression.'),
('Outcomes; Figure 2','Failure 18/26 metformin versus 12/28 control. | 69.2% versus 42.9%; full randomized denominators. | One-year conventional PFS 34.8% versus 63.0%, HR 2.42, CI 1.14–5.10. | One-year OS 47.4% versus 85.2%, HR 3.80, CI 1.49–9.73. | Wide intervals; primary Fisher P=.05. Reported failure-free difference −26.4 points has CI −51.0 to −0.9 when endpoints are ordered.','The unfavorable direction is important despite limited precision.'),
('Table 2; Results','One pre-treatment withdrawal per arm; discontinuation counted in primary failure. | Composite chosen partly to reduce informative censoring from inability to return. | Only six completed one year of metformin. | No randomized participant excluded from efficacy ITT. | 54 for efficacy; 52 started treatment. | Individual patient.','Primary composite captures feasibility, while survival analyses answer separate questions.'),
('Table 3; treatment delivery','Only grade 3 or higher adverse events recorded. | Table uses randomized denominators 26 and 28 although one per arm never treated. | Any grade 3+ AE 14/26 versus 7/28; lung infection six versus zero, esophagitis five versus one; one grade 5 respiratory failure in metformin arm. | Metformin stopped for toxicity in three; chemoradiation completion also lower in metformin arm. | Most events occurred in first 84 days; follow-up extended beyond treatment.','Do not compare these severe-event rates with all-grade rates from other trials.'),
('Methods; Discussion','Small, early-closed open-label trial, with masked adjudication. | Metformin group had larger mean tumor volumes and fewer durvalumab recipients; chance imbalance may matter. | GAP: Separate protocol and supplement not acquired; no prespecified stopping rule for accrual or efficacy reported. | Several secondary survival comparisons; exploratory results require caution. | Intervals wide but unfavorable. | Main text sample-size target 94 versus abstract/planned 96; safety denominator differs from stated treated-population approach.','Do not claim a proven biological mechanism for harm.'),
('Eligibility; Discussion','Nondiabetic unresected stage III NSCLC under intensive chemoradiation. | Mostly cisplatin-based combinations with prolonged metformin. | No support for routine anticancer addition; possible harm. | Not a test of continuing medically indicated diabetes treatment or all cancer types.','Population and regimen boundaries are essential.'),
('NCT02115464; NRG comparison','OCOG-ALMERA independent RCT. | NRG-LU001 provides a separate null trial. | Different participants and treatment duration. | Current guide inventory only.','Do not call the studies identical replications.'),
('Disclosures; funding','Theodoros Tsakiridis, Gregory Pond and colleagues. | OCOG and Canadian oncology centers. | CIHR grant; OCOG sponsored design, conduct, analysis and publication. | Sandoz Canada and Teva Canada supplied metformin. | Investigator grants, advisory fees and a family Roche employment/stock relationship disclosed.','Supply and funding are recorded without inferring invalidity.'),
('Full article','Published JAMA Oncology 2021, DOI 10.1001/jamaoncol.2021.2328. | Full text and tables acquired. | GAP: Separate protocol and supplementary outcomes not recovered. | NCT02115464 identified; historical registry not independently audited. | No correction/retraction identified in retrieved records. | November 3–4, 2026.','Assessment is source-limited but primary findings are explicit.'),
('G-METFORMIN lung-cancer chapter','Cancer treatment benefit and harm. | Contradicts routine addition; supports highlighting severe toxicity and unfavorable survival. | Trial observations do not establish the authors\' speculative causal explanations.','Do not bury harm under mechanistic enthusiasm.'),
('Synthesis','This small trial raises a meaningful warning about this combination. | Exact contribution of toxicity, treatment interruption and baseline imbalance is uncertain. | Any future regimen requires direct controlled efficacy and safety evidence.','The trial does not justify general claims that metformin promotes all cancers.')
], 'Primary failure, conventional PFS, OS and severe toxicity all favored control. Small size and imbalances limit causal detail, but the results must be prominent in a guide considering anticancer use.')

compact('COND-C15-MA32-2022','MA.32: a large negative breast-cancer trial despite metabolic effects',[
('Objectives; Methods','Test adjunctive metformin for invasive disease-free survival after operable breast cancer. | Final primary population hormone-receptor positive after planned futility assessment of receptor-negative disease. | Recurrence, new invasive cancer or death.','Subgroup analyses cannot replace the primary result.'),
('Population; Table 1','Adults 18–74 with high-risk nonmetastatic breast cancer without diabetes. | Canada, US, UK and Switzerland; complete resection and standard cancer therapy. | 3649 randomized: 1824 metformin and 1825 placebo; primary ER/PgR-positive group 1268 and 1265.','Almost all participants were women.'),
('Intervention','Apotex metformin and matching placebo. | Oral 850 mg daily for four weeks then twice daily, intended five years. | Standard endocrine, chemotherapy, radiation and targeted therapy as indicated. | Median primary follow-up 96.2 months.','Not metformin monotherapy for cancer.'),
('Randomization','Identical-appearing placebo. | 1:1 minimization, balanced by center and stratified by receptors, BMI and chemotherapy. | Computer-based allocation procedure described as minimization with equal probabilities. | Double-blind. | Concurrent enrollment 2010–2013, data cutoff October 2020.','Randomization and long follow-up are major strengths.'),
('Trial endpoints; sample size','Primary invasive disease-free survival; OS and recurrence endpoints secondary. | Centrally verified events and time-to-event analysis. | Final follow-up approximately eight years. | Clinical cancer outcomes. | Design targeted about four percentage points at five years, based on investigator consensus.','Metabolic improvements are not the same endpoint.'),
('Table 2; Figure 2','Primary events 234/1268 metformin versus 231/1265 placebo. | 18.5% versus 18.3% crude observed event proportions. | Incidence 2.78 versus 2.74 per 100 patient-years; HR 1.01, CI 0.84–1.21, P=.93. | OS HR 1.10, CI 0.86–1.41; receptor-negative iDFS HR 1.01, CI 0.79–1.30. | No primary benefit; exploratory HER2-positive signal lacked a significant HER2-by-treatment interaction.','Do not present HER2 subgroup benefit as established.'),
('Procedures; continuation','All randomized participants included in assigned-group analyses. | Missing dates imputed within known intervals; time-to-event censoring used. | Final-year reported continuation 64.3% metformin versus 70.9% placebo in the defined eligible subset. | Receptor-negative participants stopped study drug for futility while blinded follow-up continued. | ITT, with prespecified/supervised change in primary population. | Individual participant.','Discontinuation affects exposure but does not create a second trial.'),
('Continuation and adverse events','All grade 3+ events while taking study drug; treatment-related events thereafter. | Full randomized groups 1824 and 1825; exact safety count/percentage reconciliation remains needed. | Main text reports 391 (21.5%) versus 328 (17.5%) grade 3+ nonhematologic events; the latter count does not reproduce the stated percentage with n=1825. | Toxicity could lead to dose reduction/discontinuation. | Intended five years of exposure, with later follow-up.','Guide describes greater reported severe toxicity without deriving a precise absolute difference from discordant numbers.'),
('Methods; results; limitations','Strong blinded large RCT; incomplete drug continuation and changes after interim analysis matter. | Randomization protects primary comparison; exploratory subgroups remain vulnerable to chance. | GAP: A full original protocol/SAP was not recovered. The two acquired metabolic-companion supplements are an interaction table and reporting summary, not the protocol/SAP. | Primary alpha .037; secondary and subgroup findings exploratory. | CI excludes the originally targeted large relative benefit, but smaller effects remain possible. | Abstract diarrhea percentages conflict with Results; placebo grade 3+ count and percentage conflict; no silent numeric repair.','Use verified primary outcome rather than discordant harm percentages for precise effect calculations.'),
('Eligibility; Discussion','High-risk operable breast cancer without diabetes. | Five-year intended adjunctive regimen. | Does not support adding metformin to prevent invasive recurrence. | Cannot determine diabetes-specific benefit or every molecular subgroup.','Do not use a cancer null result to advise stopping diabetes care.'),
('NCT01101438; metabolic companion','CCTG MA.32 single randomized family. | COND-C15-MA32-METABOLIC reports six-month metabolic markers. | Companion participants overlap; no extra trial count. | Current source reconciliation, not an exhaustive breast-cancer review.','Metabolic change is not independent survival replication.'),
('Byline; funding; disclosures','Pamela Goodwin, Bingshu Chen and collaborators. | CCTG international trial. | Cancer charities and public research grants; CCTG trial sponsorship. | Apotex donated metformin and placebo. | Multiple investigators disclosed consulting, grants and industry employment/advisory relationships.','The main result is assessed from design and data, not sponsor labels.'),
('Full report; legacy packet','Published JAMA 2022, DOI 10.1001/jama.2022.6147. | Full main article acquired; primary table assessed. | GAP: Full original protocol/SAP not recovered; the metabolic-companion interaction table and reporting summary do not replace it. | NCT01101438 identified; no complete historical registry audit. | No formal correction located in bounded current search; internal numerical discrepancies retained. | November 3–4, 2026.','Do not label unresolved source inconsistencies corrected.'),
('G-METFORMIN breast-cancer chapter','Recurrence prevention versus metabolic improvement. | Strong direct evidence against the proposed large general adjuvant benefit. | Author conclusion and primary results agree; favorable exploratory subgroups remain hypotheses.','Keep primary and exploratory findings visibly separate.'),
('Synthesis','Metformin did not improve invasive disease-free survival in the primary population. | Smaller effects and prospectively defined subgroups remain separate questions. | Replicated subgroup-specific randomized evidence with clinical outcomes would be required.','Improved insulin or weight measures alone are insufficient.')
], 'Large, blinded randomized evidence with long follow-up is negative for the primary clinical endpoint. Metabolic companion results and exploratory HER2 findings do not overturn it. Discordant adverse-event numbers are explicitly retained rather than silently recalculated.')
